@[limine page]
Limine is a small, modern boot loader for BIOS and UEFI. It isn't on this ISO:
cast it with network (see @[todo boot-loader]). Its spell depends on llvm
(clang), which is a long compile on a fresh system; it also needs llvm's
linker lld, which its spell doesn't ask for, so cast llvm with lld first
(@[cast -r llvm], answer yes to "Build the LLVM linker (lld)?"). When casting
limine, choose the ports you need: uefi-x86-64 (UEFI) and/or bios. llvm's
last steps link clang's tools with several GB of RAM each, one per compile
job: with less than 16 GB of RAM, add swap first (a swap file is enough,
see @[guide disks]) or the linker is killed hours into the build ("ld
terminated with signal 9").

Limine only reads FAT: the kernel must be on the EFI system partition. The
simplest layout is the EFI partition mounted at /boot instead of /boot/efi
(the disk-mount step mentions it; if it is already mounted at /boot/efi,
move it and change the fstab line before installing the kernel). About
15 MiB per kernel (more with an initramfs): 512 MiB holds plenty, 1 GiB
leaves room. The commands below assume that layout, and a UEFI machine.

In @[enchantment chroot]:
 @[mkdir -p /boot/EFI/limine]
 @[cp /usr/share/limine/BOOTX64.EFI /boot/EFI/limine/]
 @[blkid -s PARTUUID -o value /dev/sda2]   # your root partition's PARTUUID
Write /boot/limine.conf (with your kernel version and that PARTUUID):
  timeout: 3
  /Source Mage
      protocol: linux
      path: boot():/vmlinuz-6.18.59
      cmdline: root=PARTUUID=PUT-IT-HERE rw
Register it with the firmware (the disk and the EFI partition's number):
 @[efibootmgr --create --disk /dev/sda --part 1 --label Limine \]
 @[  --loader '\EFI\limine\BOOTX64.EFI']
 @[exit]

BIOS machines: @[limine bios-install /dev/sda] and copy
/usr/share/limine/limine-bios.sys to /boot (which must be FAT here too).
The limine spell also installs /boot/limine/mk-limine-cfg.sh, which writes a
limine.conf from the kernels in /boot; check its root= before using it.
After each new kernel, add it to limine.conf.

Tested on hardware (UEFI laptop, EFI partition at /boot). The kernel only
panics with "VFS: Unable to mount root fs" if the cmdline lacks the
"root=PARTUUID=" part: check that line first.

